  "subdivision_of_building": "The proposed development relates solely to the rear (and part infill) section of the existing dwellinghouse. No alterations are proposed to the principal (front) elevation or wider external envelope beyond the rear-facing extension works.\n\nThe works comprise the erection of a single-storey rear extension and infill extension, together with associated internal reconfiguration. Interventions are limited to the rear elevation, rooflight insertions, and the formation of lightwells to serve the reconfigured layout.\n\nAll other parts of the building will remain unchanged.",
  "ward": "Cricklewood & Mapesbury",
  "borough": "Brent",
  "id": "Brent-26_0726",
  "appeal_decision": null,
  "street_name": "Dartmouth Road",
  "secondary_street_name": null,
  "lpa_name": "Brent",
  "date_building_work_completed_under_previous_permission": null,
  "title_number": null,
  "description": "The Applicant seeks full planning permission for the erection of a single-storey rear extension, alongside associated internal reconfiguration works to the existing dwellinghouse.\n\nThe proposed development will facilitate a comprehensive reorganisation of the internal layout to improve the functionality and residential amenity of the property. The revised arrangement will comprise two bathrooms, two bedrooms, a study area, a utility room, and a generous open-plan kitchen/living space. The scheme also incorporates an outdoor lightwell to enhance natural daylight penetration to the lower ground level.\n\nTo the rear elevation, the single-storey extension will feature high-quality glazed bi-fold doors serving the kitchen/living area, providing direct access to the rear garden and strengthening the indoor-outdoor relationship. In addition, a separate external door and window are proposed to serve ancillary spaces.\n\nThe rear-facing bedroom will benefit from a timber pergola structure, contributing positively to the usability and visual amenity of the private garden space.\n\nAt roof level, the proposal includes a carefully considered decorative treatment to the rear roof plane, introducing a periodic design feature intended to enhance the architectural character and visual interest of the host dwelling when viewed from the rear.\n\nThe infill extension element will incorporate a linear lightwell, further improving daylighting to internal spaces. The main kitchen/living area will also benefit from a roof lantern, providing additional natural light and reinforcing the sense of openness within the proposed layout. Both area will incorporate a new green roof to improve the visual impact and amenity.\n\nOverall, the development has been designed to remain subordinate to the host dwelling while delivering a high-quality residential environment in accordance with relevant planning policies and design guidance.",
